<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Roman von Ungern-Sternberg</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1885">1885</a>–<a href="/years/1921">1921</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Baron Ungern</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Roman von Ungern-Sternberg was a Baltic German nobleman and White Russian general in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-civil-war">Russian Civil War</a>. His Asiatic Cavalry Division drove the Chinese out of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulaanbaatar">Urga</a> in February 1921 and restored the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogd-khan">Bogd Khan</a>, while he ruled <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongolia">Mongolia</a> with notorious brutality. Defeated by Mongolian and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> forces later that year, he was captured and executed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> in September 1921.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/roman-von-ungern-sternberg.jpg" alt="Roman von Ungern-Sternberg" width="320" height="417" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Roman_Ungern_von_Sternberg.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Ungern-Sternberg was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/graz">Graz</a> to a noble Baltic German family that had settled in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a> in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/middle-ages">Middle Ages</a>. His first language was German, and he became fluent in French, Russian, English and Estonian. His parents divorced in 1891 and he grew up on the estate of his mother's second husband near Reval (Tallinn). He claimed descent from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/batu-khan">Batu Khan</a>, a grandson of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/genghis-khan">Genghis Khan</a>, and this played a part in his dream of reviving the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mongol-empire">Mongol Empire</a>.</p><p>In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/first-world-war">First World War</a> he served with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossack</a> regiments on the Eastern Front and won several Russian decorations, but he was also known as reckless and unstable and was sentenced to two months in prison after a drunken attack on another officer in October 1916.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>An ultraconservative monarchist, Ungern saw the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/february-revolution">February Revolution</a> as the beginning of the end of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. In the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a> he met the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cossacks">Cossack</a> captain Grigory Semyonov, and after the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/october-revolution">October Revolution</a> the two declared allegiance to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/house-of-romanov">Romanov dynasty</a> and joined the White forces in the Transbaikal region. Semyonov refused to recognise Admiral <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/alexander-kolchak">Alexander Kolchak</a> and was supported by the Japanese. Ungern believed monarchs were accountable only to God and wanted the monarchy restored as before 1905. In August 1920 he broke with Semyonov and led his Asiatic Cavalry Division, about 1,500 men, into <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/outer-mongolia">Outer Mongolia</a>. Assaults on <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulaanbaatar">Urga</a> in October and November 1920 failed with heavy losses.</p><p>On 21 February 1921, Ungern, with Mongolian lamas and princes, brought the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bogd-khan">Bogd Khan</a> back to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ulaanbaatar">Urga</a>, and on 22 February Mongolia was proclaimed an independent monarchy. The Bogd Khan gave Ungern the title darkhan khoshoi chin wang. During his five-month occupation he kept order in the capital by fear and brutal violence against <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bolsheviks">Bolsheviks</a> and Chinese. Soviet, Far Eastern Republic and Mongolian forces advanced against him in 1921. He was defeated between 11 and 13 June near Troitskosavsk, and the Red and Mongol forces entered Urga on 6 July.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>He was taken prisoner by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/red-army">Red Army</a> and tried for counter-revolution in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/novosibirsk">Novonikolayevsk</a>, now Novosibirsk. After a six-hour show trial he was executed on 15 September 1921. His attraction to Vajrayana Buddhism and his violence towards enemies and his own men earned him the names the Mad Baron and the Bloody Baron. The historian John Jennings argues that his Mongolian campaign removed the one force in the region that might have matched the Red Army and made a Soviet invasion inevitable. Ossendowski's book Beasts, Men and Gods, published in 1922, made his story widely known.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Roman von Ungern-Sternberg” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1921 the White Russian <b>Baron Ungern-Sternberg</b> expelled the Chinese and restored the Bogd Khan, before the Mongolian People's Party and the Red Army took Urga that July.</blockquote><a href="/state/bogd-khanate-of-mongolia">Bogd Khanate of Mongolia</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/graz">Graz</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/austria">Austria</a>. Died in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/novosibirsk">Novosibirsk</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/russia">Russia</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=47.07,15.44&amp;year=1908" aria-label="Graz on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4912 511 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Roman von Ungern-Sternberg"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5362 781h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5362 781h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=47.07,15.44&amp;year=1908">What was here in 1908? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
